      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1889, Whitesville became a key station on the Owensboro, Falls of the Rough and Green River Railroad.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://wiki.historyofowensboro.com/index.php?title=Whitesville,_Kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Kentucky Motor Speedway, built in 1960, added a figure‑8 infield in 1981 and launched the careers of NASCAR stars like Darrell Waltrip.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whitesville%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Whitesville Historical Society, founded in 2016, preserves artifacts, restores the Stinnett House, and supports local log cabin projects.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.whitesvillehistoricalsociety.com/",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Passenger trains stopped running through Whitesville in 1941, after which the paved Kentucky Route 54 made cars the main travel mode.",
